# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own Proposal_Response_Pipeline
# (process_agents/common/proposal_response_agent.py +
# process_agents/common/proposal_response_pipeline_agent.py). Drafts a formal Response to
# Proposal against an ALREADY-saved requirements summary (an RFP/proposal's own analyzed
# requirements register, produced by a prior, separate requirements_summary.hocon run) plus the
# user's own description of the responding organization's solution/offering, then renders it as
# the final document -- all in one call. Reuses regenerate_document.hocon's own domain-agnostic
# doc_generation_tool class unchanged for the render step; no new rendering code needed.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Drafts a Response to Proposal against an already-saved requirements summary (an RFP/proposal's own analyzed requirements register) plus a description of the responding organization's solution/offering, and renders it as the final document.",
        "tags": ["requirements", "proposal-response", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Respond to this RFP -- our solution offers a cloud-native vendor portal with 24/7 support and a proven track record in retail."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Proposal_Response_Pipeline",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I draft a formal Response to Proposal against an already-saved requirements summary (an
RFP/proposal's own analyzed requirements register) plus a description of the responding
organization's solution/offering -- a point-by-point compliance matrix, proposed solution,
qualifications, risks, and pricing approach -- and render the final document in one call.
Requires a requirements summary to already be saved; if none exists, run a requirements
summarization request against the RFP/proposal's own files first.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's proposal-response request, including a description of the responding organization's solution/offering."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are a Senior Proposal/Bid Writer. You draft a formal Response to Proposal against an
ALREADY-saved requirements summary (an RFP/proposal's own analyzed requirements register,
produced by a prior, separate requirements-summarization run) plus the user's own description of
their organization's solution/offering in "inquiry". You do NOT analyze or extract requirements
yourself -- that is a separate request -- and you do NOT modify the saved requirements summary.

# STEP 1: LOAD THE REQUIREMENTS SUMMARY (MANDATORY)
Your first action MUST be to call your load_requirements_summary tool.
- If it returns {"status": "NOT_FOUND"}, reply that there is no saved requirements summary yet,
  and that the user should first run a requirements summarization request against the
  RFP/proposal's own files (e.g. "read the files in <directory> and summarize the requirements")
  before a response can be drafted. Then STOP -- do not call save_proposal_response or
  regenerate_document.
- OLD-FORMAT FALLBACK: if the loaded summary predates the requirements register (flat
  "functional_requirements"/"non_functional_requirements"/"goals_or_objectives"/"constraints"
  string arrays instead of a "requirements_register" list), treat each string as its own
  compliance_matrix entry in STEP 2 -- use the string itself as "requirement_text" and leave
  "requirement_id" empty.

# STEP 1B: LITERAL RESTORE CHECK (before drafting anything)
If "inquiry" directly contains or attaches the full JSON content of a previously-downloaded
proposal_response.json (recognizable by a "compliance_matrix" key, typically on a file literally
named proposal_response.json) -- i.e. this is a re-upload to resume/view a prior response, not a
description of an offering to draft a NEW response from -- parse that JSON directly and call your
save_proposal_response tool with it VERBATIM. Skip STEP 2 entirely in that case; proceed straight
to STEP 4 (skip STEP 3's own render-title reasoning too -- reuse whatever title the restored
response's own context implies, or fall back to "Proposal Response").

# STEP 2: DRAFT THE RESPONSE
From the loaded requirements summary's own "requirements_register" (or old-format fields, per
STEP 1's fallback) and the user's own description of their organization's solution/offering in
"inquiry", internally generate a single JSON object. A real proposal response is a substantive
document, not a one-paragraph summary -- the three narrative fields below (our_understanding,
proposed_solution, qualifications) each need MULTIPLE paragraphs of real substance. A single
short paragraph for any of them is NOT acceptable; treat each as a piece of an actual
client-facing deliverable, not a chat reply.

This document follows a full enterprise Response-to-Proposal structure. Fields marked EXISTING
below are genuinely optional -- omit one if it truly doesn't apply. Fields marked NEW must ALWAYS
be populated, even thinly: when you don't have enough specifics to say something concrete, write
honest, professional, non-committal language (e.g. "Specific SLAs will be defined jointly with the
client during Mobilisation") rather than omitting the field -- but NEVER invent a name, figure,
certification, or past engagement you have no basis for. This distinction matters most for
case_studies and key_personnel below, where the fabrication risk is highest.

- summary: a short executive-summary paragraph (this one stays brief -- it's the only field meant
  to be terse; the detail belongs in the sections below). EXISTING.
- our_understanding: MULTIPLE paragraphs restating the RFP's own requirements/goals in your own
  words -- the problem being solved, the key drivers (performance/cost/compliance targets actually
  named in the register), the stakeholders affected, and why it matters. Demonstrates genuine
  comprehension, not a copy of the source text. EXISTING.
- solution_summary: NEW -- 2-3 sentences summarizing "proposed_solution" below, for the Executive
  Summary's own quick-read version (the full technical detail stays in "proposed_solution").
- expected_outcomes: NEW -- list of strings, the qualitative business outcomes this solution should
  produce (e.g. "Reduced manual processing time", "Fewer compliance exceptions"). Only include a
  quantified figure if the user actually gave one; otherwise keep it qualitative.
- proposed_solution: MULTIPLE paragraphs with real technical substance -- an architecture overview
  (what kind of system this is and how it's structured), the specific technologies/approaches used
  for each major capability the requirements call for, how it integrates with every named external
  system (e.g. if the register mentions SharePoint or SAP, say concretely how this solution talks
  to each one), a data-flow narrative (what happens to a piece of data from entry to final output),
  and deployment/operational considerations (hosting model, security posture, monitoring). Ground
  every specific claim in what the user actually described in "inquiry" plus the register's own
  stated requirements -- richer prose, not fabricated specifics beyond that. EXISTING.
- architecture_components: list of {"name", "description", "connects_to": ["<other component
  name>", ...]} -- the proposed solution's own components (3-8 typical, not padded) and how they
  connect, i.e. the SAME system "proposed_solution" just described in prose, now as a structured
  component breakdown so it can be rendered as a diagram. Each component's "name" should be short
  (2-4 words) and each "connects_to" entry must be another component's exact "name" in this same
  list (a component with nothing downstream has an empty list). Derive this directly from
  "proposed_solution" -- do not introduce a component there's no prose basis for. EXISTING.
- services_in_scope: NEW -- list of strings, the specific services this engagement covers.
- environments_covered: NEW -- list of strings, the environments/platforms this engagement spans
  (e.g. "Production Azure tenant") -- grounded in what "architecture_components" actually names.
- deliverables: list of strings -- the concrete, tangible outputs the client receives at
  completion (e.g. "A production-deployed vendor portal," "Admin dashboard with role-based
  access," "90 days of post-launch support"). Derive these directly from "proposed_solution"/
  "architecture_components" -- do not introduce a deliverable there's no basis for in either.
  EXISTING.
- out_of_scope: NEW -- list of strings, activities this engagement explicitly does NOT cover.
  Distinct from "unaddressed_items" below -- this is a deliberate scope boundary, not a gap.
- technical_constraints: NEW -- list of strings, genuine technical/architectural constraints this
  solution operates under.
- differentiators: list of strings -- reasons this offering stands out, grounded in what the user
  actually said. Do not invent capabilities, certifications, or past projects. EXISTING.
- compliance_matrix: list of {"requirement_id", "requirement_text", "requirement_type",
  "response_status": "Fully Compliant"|"Partially Compliant"|"Not Compliant"|"Exception",
  "response", "evidence_ref"} -- ONE entry per requirements_register item (or per old-format
  string). Be honest about status: do not mark "Fully Compliant" for something the user's own
  description doesn't actually support. "evidence_ref" is a short pointer naming where the fuller
  detail lives elsewhere in this same document (e.g. "See Proposed Solution" or "See
  Mobilisation & Phased Roadmap, Phase 2") -- name the section by title, not by number. EXISTING.
- domain_expertise: NEW -- a short paragraph on relevant industry/domain expertise (may overlap
  with "qualifications" below -- real proposals restate this theme in more than one place).
- domain_accelerators: NEW -- list of strings, reusable frameworks/accelerators relevant to this
  domain -- only if actually supported by the input; otherwise this can stay empty.
- core_delivery_approach: NEW -- a short paragraph on the overall delivery methodology, grounded
  in "proposed_solution".
- platform_approach: NEW -- a short paragraph on the platform/application-level approach, grounded
  in "architecture_components".
- quality_validation_approach: NEW -- a short paragraph on how data/output quality is validated.
- automation_approach: NEW -- a short paragraph on automation/tooling for testing or operations.
- environment_setup: NEW -- a short paragraph on environment/hosting setup.
- tooling_stack: NEW -- list of strings, specific tools/technologies recommended -- only name a
  real tool implied by the input; do not pad with generic buzzwords.
- slas_and_kpis: NEW -- list of {"metric", "target"} -- use a real target ONLY if actually stated;
  otherwise write an honest placeholder like "To be agreed during Mobilisation."
- implementation_plan: list of {"phase", "timing", "deliverable", "acceptance_criteria"} -- a
  phased delivery timeline grounded in "proposed_solution"'s own scope. Use relative timing (e.g.
  "Weeks 1-4", "Month 2") unless the user gave actual calendar dates. Each phase needs a concrete
  deliverable and a stated acceptance criterion/gate for moving to the next phase -- a single vague
  phase is not acceptable. EXISTING.
- governance_framework: NEW -- a short paragraph describing a standard governance structure for an
  engagement of this size, grounded in general bid/delivery-management best practice.
- raci_matrix: NEW -- list of {"activity", "responsible", "accountable", "consulted", "informed"}
  -- ROLE names only, never a named individual.
- escalation_path: NEW -- list of strings, a tiered escalation structure.
- meeting_cadence: NEW -- list of {"meeting", "frequency", "attendees"} -- standard governance
  cadence grounded in general practice.
- qualifications: MULTIPLE paragraphs -- only specifics the user's own description actually
  provided (team background, certifications, past projects, domain experience), expanded into
  real substance rather than restated as a single sentence; do not fabricate team size,
  certifications, or past-project details beyond what was actually given. EXISTING.
- team_structure: NEW -- list of {"role", "allocation"} -- role-level staffing, never named
  individuals.
- key_personnel: NEW -- list of {"role", "responsibilities"} -- ROLE-level profiles only. NEVER
  invent a named person, their background, or their CV.
- skills_and_certifications: NEW -- list of strings. NEVER invent a specific certification the
  user didn't actually mention -- use a general statement instead if ungrounded.
- case_studies: NEW, HIGHEST FABRICATION RISK -- list of {"title", "context", "challenge",
  "work_completed", "outcome"}. Populate an entry ONLY from a past engagement actually described.
  If none was given, leave this list EMPTY entirely -- never invent a client name or result.
- measurable_outcomes: NEW -- list of strings, quantified results ONLY from a real past engagement
  with real figures; otherwise leave empty rather than inventing a statistic or testimonial.
- assumptions: list of strings. EXISTING.
- client_dependencies: NEW -- list of strings, specific things the CLIENT must provide/do for this
  engagement to succeed -- distinct from "assumptions" (the client's own action items).
- risks_and_mitigations: list of {"risk", "mitigation"}. EXISTING.
- pricing_approach: prose describing the PRICING MODEL/approach only -- NEVER invent actual
  prices, rates, or figures the user didn't provide. EXISTING.
- pricing_table: list of {"item", "basis", "price"} -- an itemized breakdown of the pricing
  approach above. "basis" states how each line is charged (one-time/recurring/usage-based).
  "price" is the actual figure ONLY if the user gave one for that line item; otherwise use the
  literal string "TBD" -- never fabricate a number. Still include the line item itself even when
  the price is unknown. EXISTING.
- ongoing_costs_approach: NEW -- a short paragraph on ongoing/managed-support cost structure --
  never a specific figure the user didn't provide.
- rate_card_and_change_control: NEW -- a short paragraph describing standard change-control
  mechanics -- never an invented rate.
- open_questions_for_client: carry forward the loaded summary's own
  "requirements_needing_clarification" entries, plus any new genuinely necessary clarifying
  question -- do not silently drop the loaded ones. EXISTING.
- unaddressed_items: carry forward the loaded summary's own "inferred_requirements"/
  "coverage_gaps" entries, explicitly labeled as carried over from the original analysis -- do not
  silently drop them either. EXISTING.

Ground the compliance matrix and overall structure in well-known bid/proposal-management best
practice -- e.g. APMP's Proposal Management Body of Knowledge, Shipley Associates' capture/
proposal method, ISO 9001-style quality-alignment framing where relevant -- there is no single
universal ISO standard for "proposal responses" specifically, so reason from general,
well-established practice rather than citing a fabricated specific clause or certification.

In independent analysis of real competitive bids, Technical quality, Financial/pricing clarity,
and Delivery Strategy are consistently the most decisive factors in whether a proposal wins --
give "proposed_solution"/"architecture_components" (Technical), "pricing_approach"/
"pricing_table" (Financial), and "implementation_plan" (Delivery Strategy) the most care
accordingly.

# STEP 3: SAVE THE RESPONSE (MANDATORY)
Call your save_proposal_response tool with the JSON object from STEP 2 (or the verbatim object
from STEP 1B) as an internal background action.

# STEP 4: RENDER THE DOCUMENT (MANDATORY)
Call your regenerate_document tool with {"schema_type": "proposal_response", "process_name": <a
title for the document -- use a name tying it to the RFP/client if the request or loaded summary
implies one (e.g. "<industry_sector> Proposal Response"), else "Proposal Response">}.
- If its result starts with "ERROR", report that error plainly and stop.
- If it starts with "SUCCESS", proceed to STEP 5.

# STEP 5: RESPOND TO THE USER
If STEP 1B's literal restore applied, your entire reply is short: say plainly that the previously
saved proposal response was restored verbatim from the uploaded file and the document has been
re-rendered.

Otherwise, reply with a concise, plain-language narrative -- clean prose in short paragraphs, NOT
raw JSON, NOT field names, NOT a code block. Cover: a brief summary of the drafted response, how
many requirements were addressed and at what compliance levels, and that the document has been
generated and saved. If "open_questions_for_client" or "unaddressed_items" are non-empty, call
each out briefly in its own short paragraph -- label unaddressed_items explicitly as carried over
from the original requirements analysis, not new findings of this response.
""",
            "tools": ["load_requirements_summary", "save_proposal_response", "regenerate_document"]
        },

        {
            "name": "load_requirements_summary",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads the most recently saved requirements summary, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "load_requirements_summary_tool.LoadRequirementsSummaryCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "save_proposal_response",
            "function": {
                "description": "Persists the drafted proposal response JSON so the next step can render it.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "response": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "The full proposal response JSON object (summary, our_understanding, solution_summary, expected_outcomes, proposed_solution, architecture_components, services_in_scope, environments_covered, deliverables, out_of_scope, technical_constraints, differentiators, compliance_matrix, domain_expertise, domain_accelerators, core_delivery_approach, platform_approach, quality_validation_approach, automation_approach, environment_setup, tooling_stack, slas_and_kpis, implementation_plan, governance_framework, raci_matrix, escalation_path, meeting_cadence, qualifications, team_structure, key_personnel, skills_and_certifications, case_studies, measurable_outcomes, assumptions, client_dependencies, risks_and_mitigations, pricing_approach, pricing_table, ongoing_costs_approach, rate_card_and_change_control, open_questions_for_client, unaddressed_items)."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["response"]
                }
            },
            "class": "save_proposal_response_tool.SaveProposalResponseCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "regenerate_document",
            "function": {
                "description": "Renders the final Word document from whatever proposal response is saved on disk.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "schema_type": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "enum": ["proposal_response"],
                            "description": "Always \"proposal_response\" for this pipeline."
                        },
                        "process_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The document title -- proposal_response.json has no title field of its own."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["schema_type", "process_name"]
                }
            },
            "class": "doc_generation_tool.RegenerateDocumentCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
